Automated Log Rotation & Cleanup Script

🎯 Title: How I Automated Log Cleanup Across Servers with a Simple Shell Script

💡 As a DevOps engineer, I’ve seen how logs can silently eat up server space and degrade performance if not managed well.
Recently, I automated log cleanup across multiple Linux servers — a small but powerful daily DevOps win 💻

🔹 Problem:
Application logs were piling up daily, consuming gigabytes of space and slowing down backups.

🔹 Solution:
I wrote a simple shell script that:

Rotates and compresses logs older than 15 days

Moves them to an archive folder

Deletes archives older than 30 days

#######################################

LOG_DIR="/var/log/myapp"
ARCHIVE_DIR="/var/log/archive"
find $LOG_DIR -type f -name "*.log" -mtime +15 -exec gzip {} \;
find $LOG_DIR -type f -name "*.gz" -mtime +15 -exec mv {} $ARCHIVE_DIR \;
find $ARCHIVE_DIR -type f -mtime +30 -delete
echo "✅ Log rotation & cleanup completed on $(date)"

######################################
🔹 Result:
Reduced disk usage by 40%, improved application performance, and eliminated manual cleanup effort.

🚀 Sometimes, automation doesn’t need to be complex — just consistent.
